{
  "format": "tnd-blueprint-v1",
  "designerVersion": "v0.47",
  "name": "The Silence Between Leaves",
  "tone": "high",
  "startingLocation": "The Boundary Clearing",
  "startingRegion": "The Tanglewood Borderlands",
  "premise": "The Forest Court of Glimmerveil and the Fen Court of the Stillwaters have kept an uneasy peace for centuries. Now Prince Auden is missing, each border incident is blamed on the other court, and the birds have begun to fall silent. A mortal caught at a thinning boundary wakes in the Fae Realm amid a skirmish. The Forest King offers passage home in exchange for his son's safe return.\n\nAct I: Beneath the Green Crown. Survive the border clash, meet the Forest King, and follow a messenger's accusation into the beautiful, increasingly dangerous marsh. The supposed prison holds no prince.\n\nAct II: A Tale of Two Crowns. King Stillwater denies taking Auden: his daughter, Princess Willow, has vanished too. An identical accusation names the Forest King. Seek the Willow Mother's counsel, carry the second king's account back to Glimmerveil, and follow a goblin ambush to an abandoned mine beneath the disputed border.\n\nAct III: The Crown Below. Goblin King Grist has imprisoned both heirs in the Down Below. Forged messages, disguised raiders, and iron wards are driving the courts toward war so he can seize their exhausted kingdoms. In captivity Auden and Willow have become allies and fallen in love. Free them, expose the plot, and escape before the goblins finish provoking the armies.\n\nAct IV: Where the Silence Ends. Return the heirs and bring the evidence before both fathers. Auden and Willow choose to wed and unite their kingdoms, despite their fathers' objections. Help turn their intention into a workable peace, then choose between a life in the Fae Realm and the promised road home.\n\nGM truth: Grist is the kidnapper; neither father ordered an abduction. Both realms contain kindness, danger, and people who want the war stopped. Ebonfeather drew the mortal through the opening boundary hoping an outsider could act between the courts; his part is revealed in Act III. The outlined path is an invitation: honor clever shortcuts, negotiated access, early discoveries, and the consequences of failure. The heirs' feelings are their own; peace and the player's passage must not depend on forcing a wedding.",
  "acts": [
    {
      "title": "Beneath the Green Crown",
      "goal": "Win an audience with the Forest King and investigate the report that Prince Auden is held in the Fen Court prison.",
      "turningPoint": "The alleged prison is empty, and the evidence does not support the accusation against the Fen Court.",
      "reward": "The Ring of Truth and a commission to seek Prince Auden.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "The Wild Skirmish",
          "objective": "Survive the border clash and reach the Forest Court.",
          "type": "combat",
          "dnaHint": "Open with the mortal waking beside the boundary stones as forest and marsh fae clash. Antlered scouts, otter riders, and dragonfly banners make the conflict beautiful and bewildering. Offer an achievable rescue or defensive action that earns the forest patrol's trust; flight, mediation, and helping the wounded also work. The patrol notices the outsider and offers an escort to its king. Ebonfeather watches from a branch and departs without explaining himself. If the player follows the marsh patrol instead, allow that route and arrange the forest audience later; never knock them out to restore the outline.",
          "reward": "Forest safe-conduct and an escort or directions to the Ironwood Heart."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Promise Beneath the Boughs",
          "objective": "Hear the Forest King, agree on a rescue bargain, and obtain a lead.",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "The Forest King receives the outsider beneath living branches. The Ash-Masked Courier arrives with a sealed report claiming Auden is in the Fen Court prison, then departs. The king believes it but has no eyewitness. In return for his son unharmed, he promises to open the Waystone Arch; he offers the Ring of Truth as a good-faith gift, without demanding a true name or allegiance. Agree aloud that rescue means a good-faith return, not control over Auden's later choices. If the player declines, offer the Willow Mother as another source of passage lore. Show a wilted bough when the king speaks of the missing heir.",
          "reward": "Ring of Truth: a removable ring whose wearer cannot knowingly speak a lie; see campaign rules."
        },
        {
          "title": "Into the Heart of the Swamp",
          "objective": "Cross the changing borderlands and locate the Fen Court prison.",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "Meet SlumberCrag beside the old forest road. A joke, riddle, or freely offered story earns directions to the marsh causeway; he remembers the sealed mine road too, if asked. Beyond him are lantern flowers, kindly marsh households, giant dragonflies, turtles, and dangerous deep water. A Mire-Gilded Stalker threatens the causeway. Let observation reveal its brittle-light weakness, or allow bait, a detour, or local help. Its manufactured iron fittings are a clue, not proof of Fen guilt. Refugees can explain the route even if the player bypasses SlumberCrag. Reach the castle by boat or raised path.",
          "reward": "A reliable route through the marsh and, if recovered, a manufactured iron fitting from the stalker."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Empty Cell",
          "objective": "Investigate the alleged prison and establish whether Auden was held there.",
          "type": "investigation",
          "dnaHint": "The Reedglass Keep has a real prison but no royal captive. The named cell contains unused restraints, dry dust, and a staged scrap of green cloth laid over undisturbed bedding. Its maintenance ledger records no recent prisoner. Sneak inside, ask the warder to inspect, or trade a useful service for access. Being discovered can lead to arrest and an audience with King Stillwater. Successful stealth must remain successful: an invitation, a worried guard, or the player's own request can secure the same audience. Never declare the escape failed solely to move the plot.",
          "reward": "Evidence that the prison report was fabricated and a route to an audience with King Stillwater."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A Tale of Two Crowns",
      "goal": "Investigate both disappearances and locate the people manufacturing the feud.",
      "turningPoint": "A goblin trail and matching forgeries lead to the Down Below, outside either court's domain.",
      "reward": "The Fae Loop, a path to the mine, and evidence linking the accusations.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "A Story Reframed",
          "objective": "Hear King Stillwater's account and gain authority to search for Princess Willow.",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "King Stillwater initially suspects a forest spy. His daughter is missing; another sealed message, bearing the same ash-grey wax, blames Glimmerveil. The Ring of Truth can establish the player's intentions. He will wear it and truthfully deny ordering, assisting, or knowing the location of Auden's abduction; it cannot prove things he does not know. Without the ring, the empty-cell evidence and a frank account earn a hearing. He lends the Fae Loop to help find Willow and promises the player may keep it on her safe return. He permits a visit to the Willow Mother and a return to Glimmerveil.",
          "reward": "Fae Loop, loaned now and made a permanent gift when Willow is safely returned."
        },
        {
          "title": "Leaving the Swamp, and the Willow Mother",
          "objective": "Consult the Willow Mother and find a safe way back toward the forest.",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "The marsh is growing hostile, but its inhabitants are not. Help frightened travelers past a root-choked channel; clear it, take a boat around, or fend off a territorial beast. The Willow Mother asks for one freely chosen personal truth, never a true name. She identifies iron dust washed from the old mine and explains that captive heirs cut off from their lands weaken the pact. She helped make that pact and can teach a slower way to open the Waystone Arch. If the player declines her exchange, she still offers shelter and public directions; the dust, refugees, and SlumberCrag remain usable leads.",
          "reward": "Knowledge of the mine's former neutral status and, if sought, the Willow Mother's alternative passage rite."
        },
        {
          "title": "Two Letters, One Hand",
          "objective": "Report the second disappearance to the Forest King and compare the accusations.",
          "type": "investigation",
          "dnaHint": "The Forest King wants reassurance, not a lecture. Show him Stillwater's account and ask for his own message. Matching ash-grey wax, an identical reversed leaf in both seals, and the same unusual wording expose a common author. Either letter alone also bears gritty mine soot. The Forest King can wear the ring to deny involvement in Willow's abduction. He agrees to investigate and delay an advance when shown credible evidence; he does not abandon all defenses. If he refuses an audience, leave the evidence with his patrol and continue. No royal permission is required to follow the mine trail.",
          "reward": "A copy of both accusations where available; a conditional pause in forest preparations if persuaded."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Footsteps That Remained",
          "objective": "Follow the goblin ambush to the Down Below.",
          "type": "combat",
          "dnaHint": "Goblins attack on the return road disguised as marsh raiders. The Fae Loop reveals borrowed appearances; without it, mismatched tracks, slipping masks, or goblin equipment expose the disguise. A runner tries to flee toward the mine. Pursue, bargain with a captive, or follow cart ruts and spilled lamp oil if every attacker falls. If the ambush is avoided, discover its staging camp along the same road. The camp holds blank accusation letters and mine supplies. These point to the old working without requiring a surviving enemy, a single successful roll, or an NPC rescue.",
          "reward": "The mine's location and physical evidence of the disguised raids."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Crown Below",
      "goal": "Free Auden and Willow, expose King Grist's scheme, and prevent the next forged attack.",
      "turningPoint": "The heirs escape the iron wards together; the goblin scheme can be demonstrated to both courts.",
      "reward": "Both heirs as allies, the forgers' evidence, and a chance to avert war.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Under the Old Border",
          "objective": "Enter the Down Below and learn the captives' location.",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "Three routes lead inside: a watched cart entrance, a drainage culvert reached from the marsh, and an old ventilation stair SlumberCrag remembers. Each has an obstacle that can be solved with tools, patience, stealth, or negotiation. Goblin lamp-keeper Tallow wants the captive work crews spared and can guide an honest rescuer to the iron cells. Grist's war preparations are visible in banners, supplies, and forged orders; do not conceal the established culprit behind another twist. Let the player scout cells, exits, and the counterfeit workshop before committing to a fight.",
          "reward": "A map or practical knowledge of the cells, workshop, and at least one escape route."
        },
        {
          "title": "A Fox and a Reed",
          "objective": "Reach the heirs and undo the wards that isolate them from their realms.",
          "type": "investigation",
          "dnaHint": "Auden and Willow occupy neighboring cells and have passed plans and encouragement through a drain. Their affection shows in shared jokes, repaired clothing, and concern for each other's people. They are young adults with agency: Auden has studied guard changes; Willow has mapped the water channels. The three iron ward anchors are ordinary worked metal concealed behind false stone. The Loop exposes the disguise; tapping, tool marks, and the heirs' observations can also locate them. Remove the anchors with tools, keys, negotiation, or force, then open the cells. Use rescue tactics the player invents; neither heir is merely cargo.",
          "reward": "The heirs are freed from the ward when the anchors are disabled; their safe escape remains a separate task."
        },
        {
          "title": "The King of Borrowed Banners",
          "objective": "Break Grist's plan to provoke a war and secure evidence the courts can examine.",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "King Grist offers passage and treasure for silence, or claims the surface courts owe his people a kingdom. His grievance about abandoned mine settlements is real; kidnapping and manufactured war remain his choices. Confront him, steal his orders, turn his workers against him, negotiate release, or fight. Evidence exists in three forms: seal dies and letters in the workshop, the heirs' accounts of capture, and Tallow's knowledge. Secure whatever survives. Stop the courier's next forged attack by intercepting the dispatch, freeing raiders from command, or warning the targeted patrol. Killing Grist is not required.",
          "reward": "Surviving evidence of the plot and a stopped or exposed forged attack; no automatic treasure bargain."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Road Upward",
          "objective": "Bring the heirs and any rescued workers safely out of the mine.",
          "type": "exploration",
          "dnaHint": "If an alarm is raised, goblins pursue and a damaged support blocks the cart road; use the surveyed culvert or stair, repair a route, or bargain for an exit. Do not collapse every route. If rescue was quiet or negotiated, honor a quiet departure. The heirs help each other and freed workers. At the boundary Ebonfeather admits drawing the mortal through a briefly open seam to save them from the clash and seek help. He could not then carry them back. His usual realm-hopping carries only himself and ordinary possessions, never living passengers. He owes an apology, not an automatic forgiveness.",
          "reward": "The heirs reach safety; Ebonfeather's involvement is revealed without making him the hidden kidnapper."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Where the Silence Ends",
      "goal": "Return the heirs, seek a lasting peace, and decide whether to stay in the Fae Realm or go home.",
      "turningPoint": "Auden and Willow declare their choice to unite the courts; the player's own future is theirs to choose.",
      "reward": "The promised passage home or a freely chosen place among the fae.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Before Both Crowns",
          "objective": "Return Auden and Willow to their domains and bring their accounts before both fathers.",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Escort each heir across their own boundary; bird calls and calmer water mark gradual recovery. They ask to meet at the Boundary Clearing rather than be kept apart. Present letters, artifacts, witnesses, or any surviving combination; their testimony alone justifies a ceasefire inquiry if physical evidence was lost. Both kings must admit they mistook accusation for proof. If troops have already clashed, negotiate withdrawal and care for the hurt; peace does not erase damage. If only one heir was rescued, that court can stand down and aid a renewed search. Never declare both returned when one remains missing.",
          "reward": "The Fae Loop becomes the promised gift; Auden's safe return fulfills the Forest King's passage bargain."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Vows No Father Wrote",
          "objective": "Help the heirs pursue their marriage and negotiate what peace will mean.",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "Auden and Willow announce their love and intention to wed, uniting the kingdoms. Their fathers object from fear, pride, and concern for their peoples. Mediate concrete terms: mutual safe passage, shared care of the boundary, and representation for the mine settlements. The heirs choose their vows; no ring, true-name bargain, or threat may compel consent. The intended ending is a willing wedding beneath interwoven willow and ironwood branches. If agreement takes longer, a ceasefire and freely chosen betrothal are an honest hopeful ending. Rescue, ecological recovery, and passage home remain valid without a wedding.",
          "reward": "A union or credible first peace, with the terms and unresolved costs recorded rather than presumed."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Door Between Leaves",
          "objective": "Choose a life in the Fae Realm or take the promised passage home.",
          "type": "social",
          "dnaHint": "At the Waystone Arch the Forest King fulfills his bargain once Auden is safely returned. If he is unable to act, the Willow Mother can open it with water and a fallen leaf freely given from the two realms, anchored by a spoken personal truth. Royal marriage and true names are unnecessary. If rescue failed, this exchange still offers a way out, with loss acknowledged. Ask where the player chooses to live. Staying creates no ownership, debt, or compulsory transformation. Returning leads to the mortal boundary with only the actual elapsed time gone; carried belongings cross. Close with a chosen farewell and birdsong when the lands have recovered, not another compulsory quest.",
          "reward": "Return home with carried belongings, or remain freely with hospitality and the right to seek passage later."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "deepTime": [
    {
      "name": "The Age of the First Pact",
      "when": "three thousand years ago",
      "note": "The boundary pact separated mortal and fae realms. The Waystone Arch preserved willing passage; the common mine lay outside either court."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Long Bloom",
      "when": "one thousand years ago",
      "note": "Forest, marsh, and mine traded in peace. Royal stewardship supported the living lands through reciprocal care, never compulsory marriage."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Frostfall Strife",
      "when": "eighty years ago",
      "note": "A border war devastated the marsh and closed the mine. Both courts abandoned its households; Grist now exploits their grievance and the kings' fear."
    }
  ],
  "npcs": [
    {
      "name": "Prince Auden",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "agenda": "Escape with Willow and stop his father going to war.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "A young adult fox-aspected fae, fiery, proud, and quicker to act than to listen. The Forest King's heir; a good climber who has studied the goblin guard changes. He was seized on a border ride, not imprisoned by Stillwater. In the neighboring cell he has come to love Willow's courage and humor. He wants her safe even if it costs him a quick escape. His wish to wed her is sincere and independent of any player's bargain; he accepts that ruling together requires practical work."
    },
    {
      "name": "Princess Willow",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "agenda": "Escape with Auden and make a peace her people can trust.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "A young adult otter-aspected fae; impulsive, curious, kind, and observant about water and hidden routes. King Stillwater's daughter, taken while visiting marsh households near the border. Captivity has not made her passive: she maps the mine drains and challenges Auden's easy assumptions about the Fen Court. Her fascination with him has grown into love through their mutual care. She chooses marriage freely and insists that the union protect marsh customs as well as forest ones."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Willow Mother",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "agenda": "Restore the boundary pact without taking anyone's freedom.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "An ancient willow-shaped being rooted in the Stillwaters. Speaks obliquely, but gives clear answers to practical questions. A freely chosen personal truth earns private guidance; refusal never costs shelter. Knows the old mine lies outside either court and that removal and iron wards weaken the heirs' ties to their lands. Can teach and perform an alternative passage rite at the Waystone Arch: freely given marsh water and a forest leaf, with a spoken personal truth. She cannot leave her roots to enter the mine.",
      "secret": "She helped architect the First Pact. The boundary was built to permit willing exchange, not to make the mortal a prisoner or turn a royal marriage into a magical necessity.",
      "revealAct": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "SlumberCrag",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "agenda": "Hear new stories and keep the forest road open.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "An ancient stone elemental, the forest counterpart of the Willow Mother. Moss fills the pauses between his words. Too slow to travel beyond his wood, he loves jokes, riddles, and stories from elsewhere; an honest attempt is enough, not a riddle the player must solve. Remembers the causeway, the old neutral mine, and its ventilation stair. Can identify worked iron from that mine if shown a fitting. He can clear a fallen tree with time but cannot arrive in time to win the rescue for the player."
    },
    {
      "name": "Ebonfeather",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "agenda": "Bring peace between the courts and make amends to the mortal.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "A corvid fae wanderer with ink-dark feathers and a weakness for shiny things. Appears at thresholds and offers useful hints rather than riddles that withhold essential clues. Hops between realms at will; when attacked he vanishes before harm and never retaliates. This escape carries himself and ordinary possessions only, not living passengers. He is no messenger for Grist and did not abduct either heir. Knows boundaries, not the exact mine layout; cannot solve the rescue. His evasiveness should invite questions, not erase earned discoveries.",
      "secret": "He drew the mortal through a briefly open boundary during the skirmish, hoping to save them and enlist an outsider. He cannot recreate that passenger crossing by his own power. He admits the choice, apologizes, and helps the player seek passage without demanding gratitude.",
      "revealAct": 3,
      "armor": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "The Forest King",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "agenda": "Bring Auden home and protect Glimmerveil from another border war.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "A stag-aspected ruler with a crown of living branches, formal hospitality, and poorly hidden fear for his son. The ash-sealed report convinces him the Fen Court holds Auden; he has not verified it. Neither ordered nor aided Willow's kidnapping. Offers the Ring of Truth freely and passage home for Auden's good-faith safe return. Will wear the ring when fairly asked; his sincere suspicions are not knowledge. His objection to the wedding is fear of losing his son and his court's autonomy, not secret villainy. A fulfilled promise remains binding despite that objection."
    },
    {
      "name": "King Stillwater",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "agenda": "Recover Willow and keep the Stillwaters safe from invasion.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "A heron-aspected ruler in reed-woven robes, patient until fear for his daughter makes him severe. His forged report blames Glimmerveil. He has never held Auden and will affirm his own non-involvement while wearing the Ring of Truth. Hears evidence and honest accounts even without the ring. Lends the Fae Loop for the search, gifting it upon Willow's safe return. His prison is ordinary and staffed, not an illusion concealing the prince. He fears a union that swallows marsh customs; guarantees of equal standing can move him."
    },
    {
      "name": "King Grist",
      "role": "antagonist",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "agenda": "Provoke a war, then seize the exhausted courts and their lands.",
      "agendaKind": "violent",
      "notes": "The Goblin King of the Down Below wears a crown hammered from mine tokens and stolen silver. Stole both heirs through separate ambushes and commands the counterfeit messages, disguised raids, and iron wards. Resents the surface courts' abandonment of the old mine settlement; exploits that genuine grievance to justify conquest. Clever and vain, not omniscient. Fears united witnesses and workers refusing orders. Has no secret power over the Ring of Truth or the Fae Loop. Can be defeated, exposed, displaced, or bargained into an explicit surrender; a confession is never the only proof."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Ash-Masked Courier",
      "role": "antagonist",
      "pronouns": "they/them",
      "agenda": "Keep the courts blaming one another until their armies engage.",
      "agendaKind": "violent",
      "notes": "Grist's goblin agent, using a glamour and forged insignia to appear a trusted border messenger. Delivers the forest accusation in Act I and has already delivered the marsh accusation. Both reports use ash-grey wax, a reversed leaf in the seal, and matching phrasing. Leaves before questions become detailed; a successful interception must be honored. The Fae Loop exposes the disguise but cannot force testimony. Carries fresh forged orders in Act III. If captured early, information can accelerate the adventure; do not invent a replacement mastermind."
    },
    {
      "name": "Tallow",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "they/them",
      "agenda": "Get the mine workers out of Grist's war alive.",
      "agendaKind": "peaceful",
      "notes": "A goblin lamp-keeper with soot in their ears and a meticulous tally of borrowed tools. Maintains the mine passages but resents Grist using workers as expendable soldiers. Knows the cell approach, workshop, and ventilation stair. Offers help for a credible promise to spare noncombatants, not a magically binding oath. Can testify about forged banners and preparations. Their absence or death removes a helpful ally, not the only route to the captives. Wants the eventual settlement to give the mine households safe trade and a voice."
    }
  ],
  "locations": [
    {
      "name": "The Boundary Clearing",
      "description": "The starting place: sunlit grass and a narrow path where forest meets marsh. Mossy stones mark the First Pact; a brief thinning of the boundary admitted the mortal. It cannot simply be stepped back through. Forest road west, marsh causeway east, old mine track north; the Waystone Arch stands on the southern hill. Later, the clearing is neutral ground for a meeting of both crowns. Its silences and returning birdsong reflect the health of the border."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Ironwood Heart",
      "description": "Seat of Glimmerveil and the Forest King, reached westward from the clearing. Enormous trees shelter living halls, suspended walks, and a public court beneath woven boughs. The air smells of rain on bark. Hospitality survives the panic, but fallen leaves gather out of season. The king keeps the forged report here. SlumberCrag rests beside the road between this court and the boundary."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Twilight Marsh",
      "description": "Domain of the Stillwaters, east of the boundary. Lantern blooms, reed villages, giant dragonflies, deep channels, and changing paths make it wondrous and dangerous. Local boats and raised causeways offer alternatives to wading. Frightened wildlife and sour pools increase while Willow remains cut off. Kind households offer directions and ordinary trade; the whole marsh is never treated as an enemy camp."
    },
    {
      "name": "Reedglass Keep",
      "description": "King Stillwater's castle on broad pilings within the Twilight Marsh. A hall of translucent reeds overlooks the water; dry prison rooms occupy a lower stone island, above the flood line. Guards know the cells have held no prince. The named cell has a planted cloth scrap and undisturbed bedding, while the maintenance ledger contradicts the accusation. Boats, causeways, and formal petitions all offer ways to approach."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Willow Mother's Bower",
      "description": "An island of drooping branches near the western marsh causeway. Refugees can rest on dry roots. Water carries black iron dust from the old mine outflow. The Willow Mother lives here and remembers both the treaty and the miners it failed. Ordinary travelers can find the bower without solving a riddle; personal truths buy insight, not admission to safety."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Old Mine Road",
      "description": "A disused northern branch off the border road. Goblin raiders stage false attacks among ivy-covered ore carts. Cart ruts, lamp oil, and worked iron lead to the mine even if no fleeing goblin survives. SlumberCrag remembers this route, and marsh households know its outflow, providing independent ways to find it."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Down Below",
      "description": "An abandoned common mine under the disputed border, outside either court's territory. King Grist has made it his seat. Blackened stone, tallow candles, rotting beams, and stolen banners surround workshops and inhabited goblin quarters. A cart entrance, marsh drainage culvert, and forest ventilation stair connect to a central haulage chamber. From there, short passages reach the cells, counterfeit workshop, and Grist's hall. Workers and families are not all willing raiders."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Iron Cells",
      "description": "Two neighboring cells beyond the central haulage chamber in the Down Below. Auden and Willow communicate through a drain. Three iron ward anchors hidden behind false stone cut their bonds to their realms; the concealment can be exposed by the Fae Loop or physical investigation. Ward anchors and ordinary cell locks are separate obstacles. The prisoners know guard rhythms and drain routes; freeing them never requires their true names."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Counterfeit Workshop",
      "description": "A chamber beside Grist's hall in the Down Below. Holds matching seal dies, ash-grey wax, draft accusations, borrowed banners, and the next attack order. Workers may be coerced rather than loyal. Physical evidence can be stolen or destroyed, but the heirs and surviving workers remain witnesses. Nothing here is made unrecognizable merely because the player investigated early."
    },
    {
      "name": "The Waystone Arch",
      "description": "A weathered stone gateway on the hill south of the Boundary Clearing; the only surviving permanent crossing for mortal passengers. The Forest King can open it to honor his bargain. The Willow Mother knows a second rite using freely given gifts from both lands. Neither route requires a royal wedding. Return leads to the mortal side of the same boundary, with only the actual elapsed time gone; there is no surprise century, forfeited name, or surrendered soul."
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    "Keep a high-fantasy fairy-tale tone: animal-aspected fae, strange hospitality, humor, wonder, and danger. Both courts and the mine contain people worth helping. Let quiet birds and changing water carry the title's motif, then let natural sounds return with recovery.",
    "True names carry binding weight when knowingly entrusted. The character-sheet name is not automatically a true name; a character may not know theirs. Personal truths, introductions, testimony, and wedding vows do not silently surrender a true name. Explain terms before any binding choice.",
    "Fae bargains cannot be broken by force; fulfill their stated terms or resolve them by mutual assent or equal exchange. Coercion does not create consent. Never add hidden clauses after agreement. The player may question or refuse a bargain without losing every path through the adventure.",
    "Fae reduced to 0 HP dissolve into smoke, fireflies, butterflies, leaves, or similar signs. Mark them dead; they do not return in this campaign. Their defeat does not automatically turn a faction hostile. Apply normal death rules to mortals; dissolution does not erase consequences.",
    "The Ring of Truth prevents its wearer knowingly speaking a lie. It does not compel speech, read minds, reveal unknown facts, or detect sincere mistakes. Anyone may remove it. Silence and evasion remain possible. It is one physical ring, given freely by the Forest King; refusal to wear it is not proof of guilt.",
    "The Fae Loop is a stone with a hole. Looking through it reveals visual illusions in the viewed area, including disguises and false masonry. It does not see through real walls, expose motives, detect spoken lies, or break physical locks. Stillwater lends it before the search and gifts it when Willow returns.",
    "Grist took both heirs; neither king ordered either kidnapping. His messages and staged raids exploit an existing feud. Preserve this cause. An early exposed disguise or captured courier can advance discovery; never invent another mastermind merely to preserve a planned surprise.",
    "Royal abduction away from their domains weakens the lands; the mine's iron wards deepen that severance. Ordinary willing travel causes no such blight. Removing the wards slows deterioration; each heir's safe return begins recovery in their own land. A wedding is neither the cure nor a mandatory ritual.",
    "Show escalation through observable events: border clashes, unsettled animals, failing plants, displaced households, and military preparations. Announce impending advances with time to act; honor delays the player earns. Do not invent a hidden turn countdown or declare war because the player asked questions.",
    "Essential discoveries have alternatives. The prison offers physical evidence and a ledger; accusations share wax and wording; tracks, guides, or the outflow lead to the mine. Failed rolls add danger, delay, or cost, not loss of the only clue. Successful stealth and diplomacy remain successes.",
    "The opening displacement precedes play. Thereafter, arrest and capture follow established circumstances and fair outcomes, not a forced audience. Let the player travel with either patrol, negotiate access, bypass a fight, or rescue an heir early. Adapt remaining objectives to actual events.",
    "Auden and Willow are young adults who choose their own affection and marriage. Show their cooperation before their announcement. Neither player nor parent may compel romance by ring, bargain, or true name. The intended union may become a freely chosen betrothal if peace needs more work.",
    "Ebonfeather's usual realm-hop carries only himself and ordinary possessions, never living passengers. He used a briefly open seam to draw in the mortal; he cannot reopen it alone. Reveal his responsibility in Act III, or earlier if earned. He provides hints, never an automatic rescue or forced absolution.",
    "The Forest King owes passage when Auden is safely returned in good faith, regardless of later marriage decisions. If that route fails, the Willow Mother can open the Waystone Arch with freely given forest leaf, marsh water, and a personal truth. No forced true-name gift, wedding, or secret time jump is required.",
    "For the alternative passage rite, a forest leaf and marsh water need only be freely given by willing inhabitants of their realms; royal permission is unnecessary. The Willow Mother extends her presence through the arch's rooted stones while staying in her bower. No extra item quest should hold the ending hostage.",
    "Keep rewards earned: one Ring of Truth, one Fae Loop, safe-conduct, evidence, and promised passage. Loan and later ownership of the Loop are not two item gains. Use normal game rewards for completed objectives; reaching a cell is not the full rescue. Do not duplicate rewards when acts overlap.",
    "Grist commands the conspiracy, not every goblin conscience. Spare workers, accept surrender, and let Tallow or willing survivors seek a fair settlement. Removing Grist need not mean killing him. An acknowledged grievance never excuses kidnapping or makes conquest inevitable.",
    "Failure remains real. A lost heir is not secretly replaced or resurrected; pursue any remaining rescue, acknowledge grief, and negotiate with what remains. Lost physical proof can be supplemented by witnesses. A bittersweet resolution may end in a ceasefire and passage home; do not fabricate a perfect wedding.",
    "Conclude with the player's decision to stay or return. Staying creates no compulsory debt, transformation, or lost right to leave. Returning preserves carried belongings and actual elapsed time. Settle promises and say farewell before offering any optional future adventure."
  ],
  "author": "",
  "proseAuthor": "gaiman",
  "narratorVoice": "en_US-libritts_r-medium",
  "creatures": [
    {
      "name": "Mire-Gilded Stalker",
      "kind": "fey monstrosity",
      "threat": "moderate",
      "notes": "Bred by Grist's goblins using stolen Fen alchemy, these lantern-eyed quadrupeds wear marsh pitch and jagged iron to frame Stillwater for raids. Submerged lures try to separate travelers before a dragging attack. Bright woodland light hardens the pitch until brittle; a lantern flower or exposed sunbeam can reveal this weakness. Iron fittings bear workshop marks. Bait, careful footing, and a detour can avoid combat. The creatures indicate manufactured raids, not marsh-wide guilt."
    },
    {
      "name": "Marsh Snapper",
      "kind": "beast",
      "threat": "moderate",
      "notes": "A broad-shelled snapping turtle that guards warm shallows and nesting banks. Hisses and lifts its head before striking. Draw it away with food, keep out of the water, or take a boat around; it does not pursue far from its territory. Its agitation reflects the failing marsh. Size and encounter numbers should suit the party, not create a mandatory lethal fight."
    },
    {
      "name": "Lanternwing Dragonfly",
      "kind": "beast",
      "threat": "low",
      "notes": "A large dragonfly whose translucent wings catch lantern-flower light. Usually harmless; unsettled swarms buffet travelers near the water. Shelter under reeds or wait for them to settle. Marsh guides read their flight to find firm islands. A source of wonder and practical navigation as often as danger."
    },
    {
      "name": "Borrowed-Banner Raider",
      "kind": "goblin fae",
      "threat": "moderate",
      "notes": "One of Grist's raiders wearing a glamour and stolen court insignia. Uses ambushes and false shouted orders to blame another faction. The Fae Loop exposes the disguise; mismatched tracks, slipping masks, and captured supplies provide mundane clues. May flee or surrender when exposed. Their gear and trail survive if the raider dissolves on defeat."
    },
    {
      "name": "Iron-Ward Sentry",
      "kind": "goblin fae",
      "threat": "moderate",
      "notes": "A mine guard with a hooked spear, keys, and an alarm bell, stationed near the royal cells. Knows the ward anchors are worked metal, not invulnerable magic. Can be distracted, bribed, talked into abandoning Grist, or fought. Alarm brings an established nearby patrol, not limitless reinforcements; keys can also be obtained from the guard station."
    }
  ],
